## 5. Work-From-Home Guidelines and Expectations

### 5.1 Working Hours and Availability

**Standard Hours:**
- WFH employees are expected to work the same hours as office employees (9:30 a.m.–6:30 p.m. IST)
- Attendance should be recorded in the HRIS system (check in at 9:30 a.m., check out at 6:30 p.m.)
- Expected to be available for meetings and synchronous collaboration

**Asynchronous Communication:**
- If a meeting is called or an urgent message is sent, response within 30–60 minutes is expected
- Slack messages during working hours should receive a response within 1–2 hours
- If away from desk (lunch break, brief meeting), status should be updated on Slack

**Flexibility:**
- Minor flexibility on exact hours (e.g., 9:45 a.m. start if making up time later)
- No flexibility on total working hours (must still work 8 hours per day, including 1-hour lunch)
- For customer-facing roles with specific client hours, those hours must be maintained

### 5.2 Setting Up a Home Office

**Workspace Requirements:**
- Dedicated workspace (separate from bedroom if possible; not on bed or couch)
- Quiet environment for calls and focus work
- Professional background for video calls (not chaotic or personal items visible)
- Privacy: Ensure family members don't interrupt during work hours

**Ergonomics:**
- Desk and chair at proper height (back supported, feet flat on ground)
- Monitor at eye level (avoid neck strain)
- Keyboard and mouse at elbow height
- Take breaks every 60–90 minutes to stand and stretch

**Lighting:**
- Natural light preferred but not hitting the screen (causes glare)
- Supplemental desk lamp if natural light is insufficient
- Proper lighting reduces eye strain and fatigue

### 5.3 Communication and Collaboration

**Synchronous Collaboration:**
- Be present and engaged in scheduled meetings (video on for team calls)
- Avoid multitasking during meetings (no email, Slack, or other tasks)
- Proactively communicate status and blockers (don't wait to be asked)
- Over-communicate when remote (more detail in Slack messages, more status updates)

**Asynchronous Work:**
- Some tasks can be done independently; don't interrupt colleagues for every question
- Use documentation (wiki, shared docs) to find answers before asking
- Schedule focused work time without meetings for deep work
- Respect "focus hours" (times when colleagues are in deep work and should not be interrupted)

**Meetings and Video Calls:**
- Join meetings on time (especially important when remote, no "5 min late" excuse)
- Test audio/video before important calls
- Use video for 1:1s and team meetings (builds connection and trust)
- Audio-only is acceptable for larger group meetings or if technical issues

**Slack Best Practices:**
- Mark status as "online," "away," or "do not disturb" based on availability
- Use threads to keep conversations organized
- Avoid @channel or @here in large channels unless truly urgent
- Use private channels for sensitive discussions

### 5.4 Confidentiality and Data Security When Remote

**Data Handling:**
- Do not leave company data visible on your screen if others are in the home
- Close all applications with confidential data when stepping away (even briefly)
- Use privacy screen if working in a shared home space
- Never print confidential documents on a personal printer
- Use secure cloud storage (approved by IT) for file sharing; avoid personal cloud accounts

**Video Call Background:**
- Be aware of what's visible behind you on video calls
- Use a virtual background if necessary
- Keep personal items out of view
- Ensure no confidential information is visible on desk/whiteboard/monitors

**Visitors at Home:**
- If family members or guests are at home, remind them not to interrupt
- Do not discuss confidential information where others can overhear
- If a contractor or repair person is at home, secure or lock your laptop

