## 13. Frequently Asked Questions

**Q: Can I work from a coffee shop or co-working space instead of home?**  
A: You can use co-working spaces or work from public spaces occasionally, but you should ensure security (VPN, encrypted data, secure WiFi). Working from unsecured public WiFi is not recommended. Home office remains the default for remote work.

**Q: What if my family visits while I'm working from home?**  
A: You can certainly have family visiting. Just ensure you have a quiet space for meetings and confidential work. Inform your manager if it temporarily affects your availability.

**Q: Can I take a WFH day as a vacation day and not work?**  
A: No. WFH days are working days; you're expected to work 8 hours even if at home. If you want a day off, use leave (annual, casual, or unpaid leave depending on circumstances).

**Q: What if my internet goes down during the workday?**  
A: Notify your manager immediately. If it's a brief outage (15–30 min), continue working offline. If it's longer (>1 hour), document the issue and discuss with your manager; it may affect your hours worked that day.

**Q: Do I have to turn my camera on during meetings?**  
A: For 1:1s and smaller team meetings, yes—camera on is expected to build connection. For large group calls or all-hands meetings, audio participation is sufficient (camera optional). For one-on-one calls with customers or senior leaders, camera should be on unless there's a technical reason.

**Q: Can my manager randomly call me to check if I'm working?**  
A: Your manager can call or Slack you, and you should respond within a reasonable time (30 min–1 hour). Random calls to "verify" you're working would not be appropriate. If your manager feels you're not available enough, they should discuss it directly with you.

**Q: If I'm fully remote, do I have to attend all-hands meetings in person?**  
A: Semi-annual all-hands and company events: Yes, in-person attendance is expected (travel covered). Monthly all-hands: Can typically be virtual. Your manager will clarify the specific expectations.

**Q: Can I work from abroad while remote?**  
A: Short trips (1–2 weeks) are fine. Longer stays or relocation to another country require VP approval because of tax, insurance, and legal implications. Check with HR before planning extended travel abroad.

